    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost every botched sealing job we have looked at started the same way: someone bought exactly enough sealer for the label's coverage rate, ran out with a strip left, and stretched the last gallon thin to finish. That strip fails first, every time.
        </p>

        <div id="measure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Measure the Slab Properly First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage math is only as good as the square footage you feed it, and most people guess high on the driveway and forget the apron entirely. Measure with a tape, not a stride. Length times width for each rectangle, added together, is all the geometry this job needs. A driveway that is 20 feet wide at the garage and narrows to 12 at the street is two shapes, not one: measure the rectangle and treat the taper as a triangle, which is half of its base times its height.
          </p>

          <p>
            Include everything you intend to seal in one pass. The apron where the driveway meets the street, the walkway to the front door, the pad by the side gate — these are the pieces that get remembered halfway through a gallon. They are also the pieces most exposed to de-icing salt, so leaving them out because you ran short is the worst possible place to economise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not subtract for cracks, seams or the odd oil-stained patch. A cracked slab drinks more sealer than a sound one, not less, because every crack edge is exposed aggregate with far more surface area than the finished top face. If anything, a slab with visible crazing should be costed at the thirsty end of every range in this guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Write the total down and keep it. Sealer is a repeat purchase — you will be doing this again in a few years, and a measured number on a note in the garage saves you re-measuring in the cold. It also tells you instantly, next time, whether a sale price on a five-gallon pail is a deal or a pail and a half of leftovers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Real Coverage Rates by Chemistry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage varies more by chemistry than by brand. A penetrating silane-siloxane has to soak into the slab to work at all, so it is consumed by how much pore volume the concrete has. A film-forming acrylic sits on top, so it is consumed by how much surface area there is to cover and how thick the applicator lays it. Those are completely different physics and they produce completely different numbers.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Coverage (per gallon)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coats Normally Needed</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silane-siloxane penetrating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-250 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 saturating or 2 light</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silicate densifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-300 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">250-400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy garage kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-350 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyaspartic topcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-400 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Treat the low end of each range as your planning number for exterior flatwork and the high end as what you might achieve on a smooth interior slab. Manufacturers measure coverage under laboratory conditions on a dense trowelled panel; a broom-finished driveway that has been through six winters is a different material. Our own consumption on typical residential driveways lands around 150 square feet per gallon for penetrating sealers and 250 for water-based acrylics on a first coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing between chemistries rather than just costing one out, the trade-offs are covered properly in our comparison of <a href='/acrylic-vs-penetrating-concrete-sealer/'>acrylic versus penetrating concrete sealers</a>. The short version for budgeting: penetrating sealers cost more per gallon and cover less, but they go on once and last several times longer, so the annual cost is usually lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            One coverage figure that is genuinely reliable is the one printed for the specific product you have chosen, read as a range rather than a promise. If a label gives a single number with no range, assume it is the best case and plan at roughly two thirds of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="texture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Texture and Porosity Change Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Surface texture is the single biggest variable in how much sealer a slab consumes. A heavy broom finish has visibly more surface than a steel-trowelled one — the ridges and valleys are real area, and a film-forming sealer has to cover all of it. Expect a broom finish to consume 20 to 30 percent more product than the label's smooth-surface figure, and an exposed aggregate or heavily textured stamped surface to consume more still.
          </p>

          <p>
            Porosity matters even more for penetrating sealers, because those are absorbed rather than spread. Age, mix design, finishing technique and weathering all change how thirsty a slab is. The practical test is simple: flick a cup of water onto a clean dry patch. If it beads and sits, the slab is dense or still sealed and will take product near the high end of the coverage range. If it darkens and disappears within a minute or two, the slab is open and will drink at the low end.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TEST THAT SETS YOUR NUMBER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Seal one square yard before you buy the rest.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Measure out a known amount of sealer, apply it to a marked nine square feet at the coverage you intend, and see whether it looks right. Scale that up and you have a coverage rate for your slab rather than someone else's. It costs one trip to the store and saves two.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Old weathered concrete is almost always thirstier than it looks. A driveway that has been unsealed for a decade has lost the fine paste at the surface to rain and freeze-thaw, leaving the sand and aggregate proud and the pore structure wide open. Our guide to <a href='/sealing-old-concrete/'>sealing old concrete</a> covers what to do about it; for budgeting purposes, add a third to whatever the label suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Newly poured concrete swings the other way. A slab finished hard with a power trowel can be dense enough that a penetrating sealer struggles to get in at all, which shows up as product sitting on the surface and staying tacky. That is not a coverage problem, it is a profile problem, and buying more sealer will not fix it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Coats, and the Math That Follows
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage rates are quoted per coat, and the number of coats is not a matter of preference. Film-forming acrylics are designed as two thin coats; one thick coat traps solvent and clouds. Penetrating sealers are usually applied either as a single saturating wet-on-wet pass or as two light coats within the recoat window. Getting this wrong is the most common way to end up short, because a two-coat plan needs roughly 1.6 to 1.8 times the product of a one-coat plan, not twice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason it is not exactly double is that the first coat fills the most absorbent parts of the surface. A second coat of acrylic typically covers 30 to 50 percent further than the first because it is going onto a partially sealed slab. Plan the first coat at the low end of the coverage range and the second at the high end and the arithmetic comes out close.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the working formula is: square footage divided by first-coat coverage, plus square footage divided by second-coat coverage, equals gallons. For a 640 square foot driveway with a water-based acrylic at 250 and then 350 square feet per gallon, that is 2.56 plus 1.83, or 4.4 gallons. Round up to five.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are unsure whether your product wants one coat or two, our guide to <a href='/how-many-coats-of-concrete-sealer/'>how many coats of concrete sealer</a> to apply walks through each chemistry and the recoat windows that go with them. Guessing here costs more than the extra gallon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Worked Examples You Can Copy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Numbers are easier to trust when you can see the whole calculation. These three cover most residential jobs, and the pattern transfers to whatever shape your slab actually is.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Area</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Gallons Needed</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Single driveway 16 x 40</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">640 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silane-siloxane, 1 saturating coat at 150 sq ft/gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.3 → buy 5</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Double driveway 20 x 30 plus 120 sq ft walk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">720 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water-based acrylic, 2 coats at 250 / 350</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.9 → buy 5</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-car garage floor 20 x 22</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">440 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent acrylic, 2 coats at 200 / 300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.7 → buy 4</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Patio 12 x 16, stamped</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">192 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solvent acrylic, 2 coats at 175 / 250</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.9 → buy 2</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice that every one of these rounds up to the next whole container. That is not padding for its own sake — sealer is sold in gallons and five-gallon pails, and there is no such thing as buying 4.3 gallons. The rounding is where your contingency comes from, and it is why the next section matters less than you would expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            The stamped patio line is deliberately costed at a lower coverage rate than a flat slab of the same size. Stamped and textured surfaces have deep profiles that hold product, and the sealer pools in the low points whether you want it to or not. If your patio is stamped, our notes on <a href='/best-stamped-concrete-sealer/'>stamped concrete sealers</a> are worth reading before you buy anything, because the product choice changes the coverage as much as the texture does.
          </p>

          <p>
            For asphalt rather than concrete the arithmetic is different again — asphalt emulsions are much thicker and cover far less ground per gallon. If that is the job in front of you, the <a href='/driveway-sealer-calculator/'>driveway sealer calculator</a> is set up for it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extra" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Extra to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy 10 to 15 percent more than the calculation says. On a five-gallon job that is most of a sixth gallon, which usually means the rounding has already covered you. On a large job it means an extra pail, and it is still the right call, because the alternative is discovering you are short at four in the afternoon with a half-sealed driveway and rain forecast.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a second reason beyond insurance. Concrete sealer varies slightly between production batches, particularly the tinted and gloss acrylics, and finishing a job from a can bought two weeks later can leave a visible line. Buying the whole job at once, from the same batch where possible, removes that risk entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leftover sealer keeps reasonably well if the container is sealed properly and stored somewhere that does not freeze. Water-based products are the fussier of the two: one hard freeze and the emulsion breaks permanently, leaving a gritty mess that will never lay down flat. Solvent-based sealers tolerate cold better but need to be kept away from heat and ignition sources.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep whatever is left over labelled with the date and the slab it went on. Touch-ups happen, and matching an unknown sealer to an existing one is genuinely difficult — you can rarely tell an acrylic from a siloxane by looking at a cured slab. A dated container removes the guesswork and lets you spot-treat the apron next spring without re-doing the whole driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Container Sizes and What They Really Cover
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer is sold in quarts, gallons and five-gallon pails, and the price per gallon drops sharply as the container gets bigger. A five-gallon pail is typically 20 to 30 percent cheaper per gallon than the same product in singles, which means a job that needs four gallons is often better served by a pail — you get a spare gallon on the shelf for less than the cost of a fourth single.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What a Container Covers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">150 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">1 gal penetrating, open slab</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">250 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">1 gal acrylic, first coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">750 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">5 gal penetrating, one coat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1,250 sq ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">5 gal acrylic, first coat</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Quarts are for touch-ups and test patches, not for jobs. The per-gallon price is punishing and the quantity will not finish anything larger than a set of front steps. The one good use for a quart is the square-yard test described above, when you are undecided between two products and want to see how each looks on your slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concentrates are worth checking for. Some penetrating sealers are sold as a concentrate to be diluted with water on site, and the coverage figure on the label may refer to the diluted volume rather than what is in the container. Read that carefully before comparing prices, because a gallon of concentrate that makes four gallons of sealer is not expensive at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, buy the applicator with the sealer rather than after it. A penetrating sealer wants a low-pressure pump sprayer with a chemical-resistant seal; a solvent acrylic will destroy a cheap sprayer's gaskets within one job. Costing 20 dollars of sprayer into the job at the start is cheaper than a mid-job trip to replace one that has just failed over your driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying to the Label's Best-Case Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Label coverage is measured on smooth, dense, laboratory-finished concrete. A broom-finished residential driveway typically consumes 20 to 30 percent more, and a weathered one more again. Plan at the low end of the quoted range and the job finishes; plan at the high end and it does not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Second Coat in the Math</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two coats is standard for acrylics and common for penetrating sealers. A first-coat-only calculation leaves you roughly 40 percent short, which is exactly enough to get three quarters of the way across a driveway before it becomes obvious.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stretching the Last Gallon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thinning out the final strip to make the product reach produces a band that looks different and fails years early. If you are going to be short, stop at a control joint, buy more, and continue there — a planned seam is invisible, a thin strip is not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Apron and Walkways</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The apron at the street and the path to the door are the surfaces taking the most salt and the most foot traffic. They are also the ones left off the tape measure. Measure everything you intend to seal before you price anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing Water-Based Leftovers in an Unheated Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One hard freeze breaks a water-based emulsion permanently. What comes out of the can afterwards is grainy and will not lay flat. Store leftovers somewhere above freezing or accept that the spare gallon is a spare in name only.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much concrete sealer do I need for a two-car driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical two-car driveway is 20 by 30 feet, or 600 square feet. With a penetrating silane-siloxane at around 150 square feet per gallon, that is four gallons for one saturating coat, so a five-gallon pail. With a water-based acrylic in two coats it works out at roughly 4.2 gallons, which is also a pail. In both cases the pail is the right buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is one gallon of concrete sealer enough for a patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a small patio, often yes. One gallon of water-based acrylic covers about 250 square feet on a first coat and 350 on a second, so it will just about do a 10 by 12 patio in two coats. A stamped or textured patio of the same size will need closer to two gallons because the profile holds far more product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does concrete sealer coverage change on old concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Significantly. Weathered concrete has lost the fine paste at the surface, leaving the pore structure open, so it absorbs more sealer than a newer slab of the same size. Budget roughly a third more product for a driveway that has gone ten years or more unsealed, and do a water-drop test on a clean patch to confirm before you order.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy extra concrete sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy 10 to 15 percent more than your calculation. Rounding up to the next container usually supplies that automatically. The real reason is batch consistency: finishing a job from a container bought later can leave a visible line, especially with tinted or gloss acrylics.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many square feet does a 5 gallon bucket of concrete sealer cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Between roughly 750 and 2,000 square feet depending on the product and the slab. A penetrating sealer on open concrete lands near 750; a water-based acrylic on a smooth surface can reach the top of that range on a first coat. Use the label's range, and plan at its lower number for exterior flatwork.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use leftover sealer next season?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it was sealed properly and never froze. Water-based sealers are ruined by freezing, solvent-based ones tolerate cold but not heat. Stir thoroughly, and if the product has separated into layers that will not recombine, do not use it on a visible surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need more sealer for a stamped concrete patio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Stamped and textured surfaces have far more actual surface area than their footprint suggests, and sealer pools in the low points. Plan at roughly 70 percent of the coverage you would use on a flat slab of the same square footage, and expect the first coat to take the bulk of it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

